            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal />
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation>

Your house has 3 different foundation types:



1. The original house has a heated crawlspace below the main floor. The pressure treated wood foundation walls are 2x8@16" with R28 batts &amp; the floor above is 2x8@16" and uninsulated. The heating ductwork travels through this crawlspace &amp; is uninsulated. The water/septic tanks are in this crawlspace &amp; the water/drain pipes are also uninsulated.



2. The rear addition full basement. The pressure treated wood foundation walls of this basement (except for the "at grade" walkout section along the back) are 2x8@12" with R28 batts. The rear walkout section is 2x6@16" with R20 batts (same as the upper main floor walls).



3. The right side addition is a converted porch and has a "partially heated" crawlspace below. There are 3 sections of ductwork that pass through the crawlspace wall of the original house &amp; into the crawlspace below the converted porch. It was hard to get a good look in there, however I think the crawlspace walls are an insulated wood construction &amp; and the floor above is 2x8@16" and uninsulated.



You mentioned the right addition is always cold and uncomfortable, even with the pellet stove in there. During the blower door test, I had identified ALOT of air leakage coming through those 3 sections of ductwork, which tells me the crawlspace below the right addition is not well sealed &amp; also not well insulated. There does not appear to be much space in that crawlspace, so I agree that your best way to renovate it will be to tear up the existing floor.



With the floor out - you can have someone spray foam insulate the interior of the foundation walls &amp; definitely where that foundation adjoins the original house foundation walls. You could then insulate the floor above the crawl with R28 Roxul batts (with some support below to ensure they don't fall out). If you could install some vents for that crawlspace that can be seasonally opened &amp; closed from the outside (so you can vent the crawlspace during the late spring, summer &amp; early fall) than you could remove the 3 sections of ductwork between the 2 crawlspaces and seal/insulate those 3 holes.



Savings: As the crawlspace below the right addition does not represent a minimum 20% of the total foundation area, upgrades to this section do not qualify for any rebates/incentives. However ......... the best incentive you have is to have that room feel comfortable &amp; not act as an energy drain on the rest of your house. The software only calculates about $44 of annual savings, however this is "skewed" to the low side as the air leakage &amp; "coldness" of that room are just not accurately modeled by the software. I am confident you will love that room alot more after renovating the crawlspace !!</foundation>
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 121 litre oil-fired hot water tank, co-vented together with the furnace up a chimney. You may want to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 677 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 25.4 GJ and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 80% efficient gas furnace. I have modeled an upgrade from this older furnace to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing gas furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower,  you could potentially save $ 991 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 37.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Air Leakage



Overall, your house is pretty well balanced towards having adequate air exchange to keep your house healthy &amp; safe, however there are a few locations that can be addressed:



- The crawlspace below the right addition (covered in the foundation section of this report)

- The bathroom fan in the main bathroom does not seal well (see next section on ventilation)

- The current co-vented chimney for the furnace &amp; hot water (this chimney will be eliminated in conjunction with the upgrades outlined in this report)

- The 6" combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room (I think this should be kept &amp; connected to the return air ductwork of the furnace to act as a makeup &amp; fresh air supply to the house). This air supply pipe should include a manual damper so you can increase or decrease the air flow depending on how the house feels inside (stale/stuffy/humid means increase the fresh air flow &amp; dry with no condensation on windows means decrease the fresh air flow)



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - A kitchen range hood vented to the outside

        - 2 bathroom exhaust fans

        - An exhaust fan in the laundry room

        - A clothes dryer vented to the outside



You should consider replacing the bathroom exhaust fans with ENERGY STAR rated fans that use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. You will recall the bathroom fan in your main bathroom had alot of air leakage, and perhaps the damper in that fan is broken or stuck open ? Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood, 2 bathroom fans, laundry room fan and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 5.1 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace &amp; hot water tank co-vent up the same chimney so these appliances are susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.38 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.03 per litre 

Propane - $0.62 per litre delivered, $2.46 per m3 piped to the house

Wood - $315.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.09 per bag, $256.46 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

